## Priority 2. Expand same-day emergency and ambulatory emergency care

**Rationale:** A proportion of patients attending ED require urgent assessment but not an overnight bed. Same-day emergency care (SDEC), ambulatory pathways and rapid specialty review can reduce admissions while preserving access to hospital expertise.

### Interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and feasibility | Barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **2.1 Establish specialty-specific SDEC pathways** for medical, surgical, frailty, cardiology, respiratory and thromboembolic presentations | Organisational/clinical. Rapid diagnostics, senior decision-making and treatment avoid unnecessary admission. | Medium–high. Requires senior clinicians, diagnostics, therapy support and operating hours aligned with demand. | **Moderate; observational and service evaluations generally support reduced admission, but selection bias is common.** Within 1–3 years. | Diagnostics, staffing, unsuitable referrals, lack of evening/weekend capacity. | Proportion eligible; conversion to admission; 7- and 30-day reattendance; safety incidents; patient experience. |
| **2.2 Use explicit inclusion/exclusion criteria and senior clinical decision-maker review** | Clinical/organisational. Standardises risk selection and reduces unsafe discharge or inappropriate admission. | Low. Requires pathway protocols, training and audit. | **Strongly supported by general evidence on standardisation and senior review; direct SDEC outcome evidence is moderate.** Within 1 year. | Protocol rigidity and variation in clinical risk. | Time to decision; protocol adherence; adverse events; unplanned admission within 48 hours. |
| **2.3 Create rapid diagnostic access for high-volume pathways**—for example ambulatory CT, ultrasound, echocardiography and blood testing | Organisational. Reduces diagnostic delay and overnight observation. | Medium–high, depending on existing capacity. Requires radiology and laboratory scheduling. | **Moderate; evidence is mainly pathway-specific and locally evaluated.** Within 3–5 years. | Capacity competition with planned care, staffing and out-of-hours reporting. | Time to diagnostic result; admission avoidance; turnaround time; diagnostic error or delayed diagnosis. |
| **2.4 Link SDEC with community follow-up and explicit safety-netting** | Clinical/organisational. Prevents the “discharge without support” problem and identifies deterioration early. | Low–medium. Requires booked follow-up, advice lines and shared documentation. | **Moderate for transitional and discharge interventions; direct SDEC evidence is less certain.** Within 1–3 years. | GP capacity, unclear responsibility and incomplete records. | Follow-up completed; 7-day reattendance; patient understanding; complaints; delayed diagnosis. |

**Classification:** 2.1 is a high-yield intervention, but direct causal evidence is less strong than for some individual clinical interventions. It is highly context-sensitive: SDEC can become unsafe or ineffective without diagnostics, senior decision-making and downstream follow-up.

