## Priority 3. Implement multidisciplinary frailty assessment and admission avoidance

**Rationale:** Older people with frailty experience disproportionate harm from prolonged ED waits, hospital-associated deconditioning, delirium and fragmented discharge.

### Interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and feasibility | Barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **3.1 Embed early multidisciplinary frailty assessment in ED and acute assessment areas** | Organisational/clinical. Identifies reversible illness, baseline function, goals of care and alternatives to admission. | Medium. Requires geriatrician or advanced practitioner, nursing, therapy and social-care input. | **Moderate-to-strong; comprehensive geriatric assessment has supportive evidence, while the optimal ED model varies.** Within 1–3 years. | Geriatric workforce shortages, crowding and lack of community alternatives. | Time to frailty assessment; admission rate; delirium; falls; functional change; patient and carer experience. |
| **3.2 Use early comprehensive geriatric assessment for admitted frail patients** | Organisational/clinical. Coordinates medical, nursing, therapy, medication and discharge planning. | Medium. Requires multidisciplinary staffing and protected time. | **Strong evidence for comprehensive geriatric assessment in appropriate hospital settings** (Ellis et al., 2017). Within 1–3 years. | Workforce and space; inconsistent fidelity. | Institutionalisation, length of stay, readmission, mortality, mobility and functional status. |
| **3.3 Provide hospital-at-home or acute frailty-at-home for carefully selected patients** | Organisational/policy. Delivers hospital-level treatment in the person’s home, avoiding admission and reducing hospital harms. | High. Requires rapid response, diagnostics, medicines, therapy, overnight escalation and reliable carers. | **Moderate; hospital-at-home evidence supports selected groups, but outcomes depend heavily on eligibility and service maturity** (Shepperd et al., 2016; Shepperd et al., 2021). Within 3–5 years. | Workforce, rural geography, carer burden, digital exclusion and safeguarding. | Admissions avoided; mortality; adverse events; 30-day readmission; patient/carer outcomes; equity. |
| **3.4 Introduce delirium prevention and early recognition bundles** | Clinical/organisational. Addresses cognition, hydration, mobility, sleep, sensory impairment and medication risk. | Low–medium. Requires staff training, equipment and ward/ED routines. | **Moderate; multicomponent prevention has supportive evidence, although implementation fidelity varies.** Within 1–3 years. | ED environment, noise, crowding and insufficient therapy/nursing time. | Delirium incidence; duration; falls; antipsychotic use; compliance with bundle. |

**Classification:** Comprehensive geriatric assessment is strong. Hospital-at-home is promising and potentially high impact, but resource- and selection-sensitive.

