## Priority 5. Improve hospital flow, ambulance handover and front-door streaming

**Rationale:** ED crowding is associated with delays, poorer experience, staff harm and adverse outcomes. Flow interventions are central to winter resilience, but isolated ED changes cannot compensate for insufficient inpatient or social-care capacity.

### Interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and feasibility | Barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **5.1 Implement rapid multidisciplinary streaming at the front door** | Organisational/clinical. Directs patients to resuscitation, minors, SDEC, frailty, mental health or primary-care alternatives according to clinical need. | Medium. Requires senior triage, trained nurses, diagnostics and clear pathways. | **Moderate; evidence supports structured streaming, but effects depend on downstream capacity.** Within 1–3 years. | Inappropriate streaming, queue displacement and staffing at peak times. | Time to first clinical assessment; time to treatment; streaming accuracy; breaches; harm events. |
| **5.2 Use ambulance handover protocols with escalation triggers and a hospital-wide response** | Organisational/policy. Reduces ambulance queuing and maintains emergency response capacity. | Medium. Requires operational command, handover spaces, clinical escalation and ambulance–hospital data. | **Moderate; evidence is mainly observational and policy evaluation.** Within 1–3 years. | Bed shortages, corridor care, ambulance demand and competing priorities. | Handover within 15/30/60 minutes; ambulance turnaround; lost hours; clinical incidents; staff injury. |
| **5.3 Introduce whole-hospital operational control with predicted demand and real-time bed status** | Organisational. Makes flow a hospital-wide responsibility rather than an ED problem. | Medium–high. Requires site management, reliable data, senior escalation and discharge coordination. | **Promising; operational studies support flow management, but causal evidence is variable.** Within 1–3 years. | Inaccurate data, lack of executive authority and incentives favouring individual departments. | ED occupancy; boarding time; time to inpatient bed; cancelled elective activity; length of stay. |
| **5.4 Use a clinically governed, minimum-data “safety record” when the full GP record is unavailable** | Clinical/digital/organisational. Ensures access to allergies, medicines, anticoagulation, renal function, diagnoses, frailty, advance decisions and recent attendances. | Medium. Requires interoperability, consent/privacy governance and fallback processes. | **Promising; patient-safety rationale is strong, but outcome evidence for specific interoperability models is limited.** Within 3–5 years. | Information-governance concerns, poor data quality and incompatible systems. | Proportion with key data available; medication discrepancies; duplicated tests; adverse drug events; clinician-reported usefulness. |

**Classification:** Front-door streaming and hospital-wide flow are high priority but context-sensitive. Digital access is promising rather than proven.

