## Priority 6. Reduce medically fit delays and improve discharge to home or intermediate care

**Rationale:** Delayed discharge reduces available beds, increases ED boarding and can cause deconditioning and hospital-acquired harm. It is a health and social-care system problem, not solely an ED problem.

### Interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and feasibility | Barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **6.1 Start discharge planning at admission, including estimated date of discharge and expected functional goals** | Clinical/organisational. Aligns treatment, therapy, medicines and community arrangements early. | Low–medium. Requires multidisciplinary documentation and daily review. | **Moderate; discharge-planning interventions improve some outcomes, but effects vary** (Gonçalves-Bradley et al., 2016). Within 1 year. | Uncertainty early in admission, poor ownership and changing clinical status. | Estimated-date accuracy; discharge before midday; length of stay; readmission; patient preparedness. |
| **6.2 Use multidisciplinary board rounds with an explicit “next three actions” plan** | Organisational. Identifies diagnostic, therapy, social-care and transport blockers each day. | Low–medium. Requires protected multidisciplinary time and escalation routes. | **Promising; much evidence is before–after or quality improvement.** Within 1 year. | Meeting burden and superficial compliance. | Delayed-day count; unresolved blockers; time from medically fit to discharge; length of stay. |
| **6.3 Expand home-first/discharge-to-assess pathways** | Policy/organisational. Assesses longer-term care needs in the person’s usual environment and avoids unnecessary institutionalisation. | Medium–high. Requires reablement, therapy, equipment, transport and social-care capacity. | **Moderate; supported by UK evaluations and related transitional-care evidence, but service effects vary.** Within 1–3 years. | Social-care workforce, housing, carer availability and rurality. | Discharge destination; delayed days; care-home admissions; readmissions; functional outcomes. |
| **6.4 Provide seven-day therapy, pharmacy and discharge coordination for high-risk patients** | Organisational. Removes weekend delays and reduces medication and follow-up failures. | Medium. Requires rota redesign and community availability. | **Moderate for selected components; evidence for universal seven-day provision is mixed.** Within 1–3 years. | Staffing costs, variable weekend demand and community closure. | Weekend discharges; medication errors; time to first community contact; readmission. |
| **6.5 Establish joint NHS–local authority winter capacity plans** | Policy. Coordinates intermediate care, domiciliary care, care homes, equipment, transport and escalation. | Medium–high. Requires pooled planning, data sharing and workforce investment. | **Strong system rationale but limited experimental evidence.** Within 1–3 years. | Separate budgets, delayed procurement and workforce shortages. | Delayed transfers; social-care vacancy rates; care-package waiting time; system occupancy. |

**Classification:** Early discharge planning and home-first are supported but implementation-sensitive. Joint capacity planning is essential yet evidence is mainly systems and policy evidence.

---

## Priority 7. Improve access to timely primary and community care, including NHS 111 and pharmacy

**Rationale:** A&E is frequently used because it is perceived as accessible, reliable and able to provide diagnostics. Simply telling patients not to attend is unlikely to work unless credible alternatives exist.

### Interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and feasibility | Barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **7.1 Provide enhanced same-day primary-care appointments during predictable winter peaks** | Policy/organisational. Offers a credible alternative for acute infections, medication problems and chronic-disease exacerbations. | Medium. Requires workforce, booking systems and clinical back-up. | **Moderate for improving access; evidence that it reduces total ED demand is mixed.** Within 1–3 years. | GP workforce, appointment fragmentation and demand substitution. | Same-day access; ED attendances by primary-care-sensitive condition; patient experience; continuity. |
| **7.2 Expand pharmacist-led minor-illness and medication services with direct referral pathways** | Organisational/clinical. Treats self-limiting illness and resolves medication problems without ED attendance. | Low–medium. Requires trained pharmacists, prescribing authority where applicable, referral protocols and information sharing. | **Moderate-to-strong for appropriate minor ailments; effect on overall ED demand is uncertain.** Within 1–3 years. | Pharmacy capacity, remuneration and clinical escalation. | Consultations; cases managed; referrals to ED; antibiotic prescribing; patient outcomes. |
| **7.3 Improve NHS 111 clinical assessment, direct booking and safety-netting** | Policy/digital/clinical. Directs patients to the most appropriate service and provides advice when immediate care is unnecessary. | Medium. Requires trained clinicians, service availability, algorithms and feedback loops. | **Moderate; NHS 111 and telephone triage evidence is mixed and may increase service utilisation in some groups.** Within 1–3 years. | Algorithm limitations, language barriers, digital exclusion and unavailable appointments. | Disposition accuracy; ED attendance within 24/72 hours; call abandonment; equity; complaints. |
| **7.4 Use population health education focused on symptom severity, self-care and escalation** | Policy/community. Improves health literacy without discouraging appropriate emergency attendance. | Low–medium. Requires translated, accessible, culturally adapted communication through trusted organisations. | **Moderate for knowledge and self-management; weaker evidence for reducing ED attendance.** Within 1–3 years. | Message fatigue, mistrust and risk of unsafe reassurance. | Knowledge measures; avoidable attendance patterns; delayed presentation; equity. |

**Classification:** Pharmacy and reliable same-day primary care are practical and potentially high-value. Public campaigns alone are weak evidence and should not be used as a substitute for service access.

