## Priority 8. Build a safer, more sustainable emergency-care workforce

**Rationale:** Winter pressure increases sickness absence, fatigue, violence, moral distress, turnover and error risk. Workforce interventions must reduce workload and improve job control rather than rely mainly on individual resilience training.

### Interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and feasibility | Barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **8.1 Implement minimum staffing and skill-mix escalation plans linked to demand and acuity** | Organisational/policy. Matches staffing to workload and provides safe escalation before crowding becomes hazardous. | Medium–high. Requires workforce modelling, bank/agency arrangements and executive escalation. | **Moderate rationale; exact staffing thresholds have limited causal evidence.** Within 1–3 years. | Workforce shortages, financial constraints and unsafe reliance on temporary staff. | Nurse/doctor fill rates; acuity-adjusted workload; sickness absence; incidents; waiting times. |
| **8.2 Provide protected rest, hydration, meal and recovery arrangements** | Organisational. Reduces fatigue-related error and improves worker safety and retention. | Low–medium. Requires rota and operational redesign. | **Moderate occupational-health evidence; direct ED winter outcome data limited.** Within 1 year. | Cultural normalisation of missed breaks and workload intensity. | Missed-break rate; fatigue scores; sickness absence; staff incidents; turnover. |
| **8.3 Implement evidence-based violence-prevention systems**: environmental review, security response, de-escalation training and post-incident support | Organisational/policy. Reduces assault risk and supports reporting and recovery. | Medium. Requires security, training, environment and incident governance. | **Moderate; multicomponent approaches are more plausible than training alone.** Within 1–3 years. | Under-reporting, limited security resources and poor learning from incidents. | Assaults; staff injuries; reporting rate; response time; staff perception of safety. |
| **8.4 Use structured team briefings, simulation and human-factors training for high-risk winter processes** | Clinical/organisational. Improves shared mental models, escalation and reliability during surges. | Low–medium. Requires educator time and protected training. | **Moderate for teamwork behaviours; direct patient-outcome evidence is variable.** Within 1–3 years. | Training release time and tokenistic implementation. | Training completion; escalation reliability; safety climate; critical-event learning. |
| **8.5 Offer confidential, rapid-access occupational mental-health support** | Organisational. Supports staff with distress, trauma and burnout and may improve retention. | Medium. Requires occupational health, confidentiality and rapid appointments. | **Moderate for staff wellbeing; effects on patient outcomes and retention vary.** Within 1–3 years. | Stigma, lack of trust and insufficient occupational-health capacity. | Uptake; wellbeing/burnout scores; sickness absence; retention; psychological safety. |

**Classification:** Multicomponent violence prevention and protected rest are undervalued because they are less visible than new clinical pathways, despite their likely effect on safety and retention. Individual resilience training alone is insufficient.

