## Priority 9. Improve medicines safety, antimicrobial stewardship and diagnostic stewardship

**Rationale:** Winter demand increases prescribing, polypharmacy, diagnostic uncertainty and transitions between services. Avoiding low-value tests and unsafe medicines can improve effectiveness, safety, efficiency and environmental sustainability.

### Interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and feasibility | Barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **9.1 Pharmacist-led medicines reconciliation within 24 hours for high-risk patients** | Clinical/organisational. Detects omissions, duplications, anticoagulant errors and renal-dose problems. | Medium. Requires clinical pharmacists, access to GP/community records and prioritisation tools. | **Moderate-to-strong for detecting discrepancies; effects on admissions/readmissions vary.** Within 1–3 years. | Pharmacy workforce and incomplete medication records. | Reconciliation within 24 hours; discrepancies; medication-related incidents; readmission. |
| **9.2 Antimicrobial stewardship bundles for respiratory and urinary presentations** | Clinical/organisational. Improves diagnostic justification, antibiotic choice, duration and review. | Low–medium. Requires guidelines, prescribing feedback, microbiology and decision support. | **Strong evidence that stewardship interventions improve prescribing; patient-outcome effects depend on design** (Davey et al., 2017). Within 1–3 years. | Diagnostic uncertainty, time pressure and fear of missing sepsis. | Antibiotic initiation; guideline concordance; duration; C. difficile; resistance; reattendance. |
| **9.3 Use diagnostic stewardship for urine cultures, chest imaging and blood tests** | Clinical/organisational. Reduces false positives, incidental findings, iatrogenic anaemia and unnecessary treatment. | Low–medium. Requires criteria, order sets and feedback. | **Moderate for selected tests; inappropriate testing is common, but outcome evidence is pathway-specific.** Within 1–3 years. | Defensive medicine, clinician autonomy and concern about delayed diagnosis. | Tests per attendance; repeat testing; abnormal-result follow-up; diagnostic error; carbon/resource use. |
| **9.4 Embed medication and diagnostic decision support into electronic ordering systems** | Digital/clinical. Provides renal-dose, allergy, interaction, guideline and appropriateness prompts at the point of care. | Medium–high. Requires interoperable systems, governance and usability testing. | **Moderate for prescribing safety; alert fatigue and variable effectiveness are recognised.** Within 3–5 years. | Poor integration, excessive alerts and unreliable data. | Override rates; serious prescribing errors; antibiotic appropriateness; test utilisation. |

**Classification:** Antimicrobial stewardship is strong. Broad diagnostic stewardship is promising but should use condition-specific measures and must not reward under-testing.

