## Priority 10. Establish interoperable information, risk stratification and learning systems

**Rationale:** Emergency clinicians frequently make decisions without complete GP records. Better access to allergies, medicines, diagnoses, frailty, previous results, care plans and patient preferences can improve safety and efficiency.

### Interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and feasibility | Barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **10.1 Provide view-only access to a minimum urgent-care dataset** | Policy/digital/clinical. Reduces duplicated testing, medication errors and inappropriate admissions. | Medium–high. Requires interoperability, identity matching, information governance and clinician training. | **Promising; safety rationale is strong, but causal outcome evidence is limited and implementation-dependent.** Within 3–5 years. | Data fragmentation, incompatible systems, consent and poor data quality. | Record availability; clinician access; duplicate tests; medication discrepancies; adverse events. |
| **10.2 Use standardised electronic safety-netting and discharge communication** | Clinical/digital. Makes diagnosis, uncertainty, pending results, red flags and follow-up responsibilities explicit. | Low–medium. Requires templates, training and result ownership. | **Moderate for improving communication; direct reductions in harm are less certain.** Within 1–3 years. | Template burden, poor responsibility for pending results and language/accessibility problems. | Discharge-summary completion; patient comprehension; unreviewed results; reattendance. |
| **10.3 Develop transparent demand and capacity dashboards** covering ED, beds, ambulance handover, discharge delays, community capacity and workforce | Organisational/policy. Supports earlier escalation and prevents local optimisation. | Medium. Requires data engineering, definitions and daily operational ownership. | **Promising; evidence is largely implementation and operational research.** Within 1–3 years. | Data latency, gaming of metrics and dashboard proliferation. | Data completeness; escalation timeliness; forecast accuracy; occupancy; delayed transfers. |
| **10.4 Use risk stratification to target proactive intervention, with equity and safety review** | Policy/organisational. Identifies people at high risk of admission or deterioration for clinical review, not automatic exclusion from care. | Medium–high. Requires validated models, data science, clinical governance and monitoring for bias. | **Promising but uncertain; predictive performance does not automatically translate into improved outcomes.** Within 3–5 years. | Algorithmic bias, false positives, automation bias and poor intervention capacity. | Calibration by ethnicity/deprivation; intervention uptake; admissions; false-negative safety events; equity. |

**Classification:** Minimum-data access is undervalued and likely high value, but the evidence is not yet equivalent to evidence for a clinical treatment. AI and predictive analytics should be treated as decision support, not autonomous triage.

