# Evidence classification summary

| Category | Priorities/interventions |
|---|---|
| **Strongest evidence** | Comprehensive geriatric assessment; hospital-at-home for selected patients; discharge planning; antimicrobial stewardship; vaccination for prevention of severe respiratory disease; selected pulmonary rehabilitation interventions. |
| **Moderate evidence with local variation** | SDEC; frailty front-door services; pharmacist minor-illness services; medicines reconciliation; delirium prevention; structured safety-netting; primary-care access interventions. |
| **Promising but undervalued** | Staff rest and worker-safety interventions; minimum urgent-care data access; early discharge blocker removal; care-home direct referral; diagnostic stewardship. These may be undervalued because their benefits are distributed across the system and are less visible than new clinical services. |
| **Mainly case-example or service-evaluation evidence** | Many virtual wards, urgent community-response models, demand dashboards, predictive-risk systems and specific ambulance-handover configurations. |
| **Weak or inconsistent evidence** | Public education campaigns alone to reduce ED attendance; blanket expansion of telephone triage; unvalidated AI triage; universal virtual wards; isolated flow dashboards without additional operational capacity; individual resilience training without workload reduction. |

